==English==

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*kap-}}{{root|en|ine-pro|*kap-|id1=head}}
{{lbor|en|la|caput|t=the head}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*káput}} (English {{m|en|head}}), itself from {{der|en|ine-pro|*kap-}}. {{dbt|en|cape#Etymology 1|capo|chef|chief}}, and distantly of {{doublet|en|head|Howth|notext=1}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/kəˈpʊt/|/ˈkæp.ət/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-caput.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{homophone|en|kaput|qq1=for pronunciation with stress on second syllable}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|s|capita}}

# {{lb|en|anatomy}} The [[head]].
# {{lb|en|anatomy}} A [[knob]]-like [[protuberance]] or [[capitulum]].
# The [[top]] or [[superior]] part of a thing.
# {{lb|en|UK}} The [[council]] or [[rule|ruling]] body of the {{w|University of Cambridge}} prior to the constitution of 1856.
#* {{RQ:Lamb Essays of Elia|page=22|text=D. has been engaged, he tells me, through a course of laborious years, in an investigation into all curious matter connected with the two Universities; and has lately lit upon a MS. collection of charters, relative to C&NoBreak;⸺, by which he hopes to settle some disputed points—particularly that long controversy between them as to priority of foundation. The ardor with which he engages in these liberal pursuits, I am afraid, has not met with all the encouragement it deserved, either here, or at C&NoBreak;⸺. Your '''caputs''', and heads of colleges, care less than any body else about these questions.}}
# {{lb|en|medicine|colloquial}} {{ellipsis of|en|caput succedaneum}}

